In C++, you can use the `std::apply` function from the `<utility>` header to run the same function with different arguments. However, for simple cases where you want to apply the same function to multiple variables, you can use a lambda function or a function pointer with a variadic template. Here's a concise way to do it:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <utility>

// Example function to be applied
void print(const std::string& str) {
    std::cout << str << std::endl;
}

int main() {
    // Named variables
    std::string var1 = "Hello";
    std::string var2 = "World";
    std::string var3 = "C++";

    // Apply the function to each variable
    std::apply([](const std::string& var) { print(var); }, std::tuple(var1, var2, var3));

    return 0;
}
```

In this example, `std::apply` takes a lambda function and a tuple of the variables. The lambda function is applied to each element of the tuple, effectively running the same function on each variable. This approach is concise and leverages C++11 and later features.

